@style(../styles/IDocDemo.style)
@title(Small teaching widgets and Knowsy modules)

@box(w=100%, style=offline)
@@module embeds a Knowsy page, so it needs the network.
@end

Every block on this page is live in the preview. Each feature gets one line and one working example; the block is also what prints in the PDF.

@section(Flashcards with spaced repetition)

A deck of study cards, front and back, that schedules each card for review based on how well you knew it.

@flashcards(id=ohm)
What is Ohm's law? :: V = IR
Unit of resistance :: the ohm
@end

@b{Cards.} One card per line: the front, two colons, the back.

@flashcards(id=greek)
alpha :: α
beta :: β
@end

@b{Saved progress.} id= (or deck=) names the deck, so each reader's progress is kept in their own browser between visits.

@flashcards(deck=week3)
Capacitor energy :: half C V squared
@end

@b{Graded.} Not graded.

@section(Hints shown one at a time)

A ladder of hints under a question that the student opens one at a time, each optionally costing points.

Total resistance of 2 Ω and 3 Ω in series: @q(fn: 5)
@hint
In series, resistances add.
Two plus three.
@end

@b{One hint per line.} Each non-empty line is one hint, revealed in order; a leading - or * is dropped.

@hint
- Start from Kirchhoff's voltage law.
- Now sum the drops.
@end

@b{A cost.} penalty= shows the points off per hint and tells the course it was used.

@hint(penalty=1)
Think about units.
@end

@b{Graded.} A hint is not a question.

@section(Confidence-based marking)

After a question, the student says how sure they are, and the mark rewards being right and sure and punishes being wrong and sure.

2 + 2 = @q(fn: 4)
@confidence
@end

@b{The scale.} Low: right +1, wrong 0.

Capital of France? @q(f: Paris)
@confidence
@end

@b{Graded.} It reads the question just above it and shows the confidence mark live as the answer or the level changes.

@section(Follow-up shown by how a question went)

Content under a question that appears only when it was answered right, answered wrong, or answered at all.

Ohm's law: V = @q(mcd: *I R, I/R)
@branch(if=wrong)
Voltage is current times resistance. Review section 2.
@end

@b{The condition.} if=correct, if=wrong, or if=answered (the default).

2 + 2 = @q(fn: 4)
@branch(if=correct)
Well done. Try the challenge below.
@end

@b{Any content.} The body is full IDoc: text, pictures, even another question.

Pick a prime: @q(mcv: *7, 8)
@branch(if=answered)
Next: @q(f: 2)
@end

@b{Graded.} The branch itself is not graded; a question inside it is graded as usual.

@section(Choosing between versions of a question)

Place a question drawn from the document's bank by its label: one per student when several are named, or a switch the reader can flip between versions.

@quiz
Capital of France? @q(mch, *Paris, Rome, label=easy)
Capital of Bhutan? @q(mch, *Thimphu, Paro, label=hard)
@endquiz
@selectquestion(id=s1, fromid="easy,hard", toggle=lock)

@b{A draw per student.} fromid= with several labels and no toggle picks one per student seed, the same one every time for that seed.

@quiz
Q1 @q(f: 4, label=a)
Q2 @q(f: 6, label=b)
@endquiz
@selectquestion(id=s2, fromid="a,b")

@b{A reader's switch.} toggle= shows a switch between the versions; every selector with the same toggle value switches together.

@selectquestion(id=s3, fromid="easy,hard", toggle=lock)

@b{Graded.} The chosen question is graded exactly as it would be on its own; the selector adds nothing to the mark.

@section(Vector shapes)

A vector shape (box, ellipse, arrow, star, callout and more) with optional text, inline or placed anywhere on a slide.

@shape(type=star, w=140, h=140, fill=#ff8e53, text=New!)

@b{Kinds.} type= rect, ellipse, triangle, diamond, pentagon, hexagon, star, arrow, line, callout.

@shape(arrow, w=200, h=40, fill=#4c8dff)

@b{Look.} fill= and stroke= colours, sw= stroke width, rotate= degrees, text= a centred label in color=.

@shape(type=ellipse, w=120, h=60, fill=#fff, stroke=#333, sw=2, rotate=15, text=Idea, color=#333)

@b{Place it.} x= and y= position it absolutely on a slide or @@page; drag it in the preview and the new place is written back.

@shape(type=rect, x=20%, y=60%, w=200, h=40, fill=#4c8dff)

@b{Graded.} Not graded: a shape is a picture, and records no answer.

@section(Diagrams from a list)

Turn a short list into a laid-out diagram: steps with arrows, a stack, a cycle or a hierarchy.

@smartart(type=process, items=Plan; Build; Ship)

@b{Layouts.} type= process (boxes and arrows), list (stacked), cycle (a ring), hierarchy (the first item over the rest).

@smartart(type=hierarchy, items=Dean; Physics; Chemistry; Biology)

@b{Colours.} fill= the boxes, color= the text, accent= the arrows and lines.

@smartart(type=list, items=Read; Try; Ask, fill=#eef4ff, color=#123, accent=#2563eb)

@b{Graded.} Not graded: a diagram is a picture, and records no answer.

@section(Knowsy interactive modules)

Put one of Knowsy's interactive modules — a calculus explorer, a CPU animator, a bit playground and others — inside the document, running in its own frame.

@module(cpu-animator)

@b{By name or by address.} The first argument is the module's id (the @@module( menu offers them); url= takes a page name or full address instead.

@module(url=cpu-animator)

@b{Size and title.} h= sets the frame's height (460px by default); title= replaces the name in the bar.

@module(cpu-animator, h=600px, title=The fetch cycle)

@b{Keep Knowsy's own challenge.} challenge=yes keeps the module's challenge bar and navbar, hidden by default because in a document they are another site's task.

@module(cpu-animator, challenge=yes)

@b{Graded.} A module on its own is not graded.

@section(Click on a picture: hotspot questions)

Ask a student to click the right place on a picture or diagram, graded against target zones.

@hotspot(src=teaching-widgets.assets/circuit.svg, targets=#R2)
Click R2.
@end

@b{Targets.} targets= is the id of an element in an SVG (#R2) or a circle x,y,r in percent of the picture; several are separated by ;.

@hotspot(src=teaching-widgets.assets/map.png, targets=10,13,6)
Click the top-left corner of the grey frame.
@end

@b{More than one pick.} max= caps how many markers a student may drop.

@hotspot(src=teaching-widgets.assets/circuit.svg, targets=#R1;#R2, max=2)
Click both resistors.
@end

@b{Graded.} Each target zone must contain a marker, and each earns its share of pts=.

@section(Drag labels onto a diagram)

Ask a student to drag labels, pictures or parts onto the right places in a diagram.

@dragdrop(src=teaching-widgets.assets/circuit.svg)
Drag each label onto its component.
R1 -> #R1
R2 -> #R2
@end

@b{Items and targets.} Each line is ITEM -> TARGET.

@dragdrop(src=teaching-widgets.assets/board.svg)
img:teaching-widgets.assets/parts/cap.svg -> #C1
#battery -> 5/50/8
@end

@b{Graded.} An item is right when its centre lands inside its target; each item earns its share of pts=.

@section(Wire terminals together on a picture)

Ask a student to draw connections between terminals on a picture — wire a circuit, link concepts — graded against the expected connections.

@connect(src=teaching-widgets.assets/board.svg)
Wire the battery to both resistors.
node BAT @ 8,50
BAT - #R1
BAT - #R2
@end

@b{Nodes and edges.} A node is an #svg-id or node NAME @@ x,y in percent; an edge is A - B, or A -> B with directed to grade direction.

@connect(src=teaching-widgets.assets/map.svg, directed)
#a -> #b
@end

@b{Graded.} Each expected connection earns its share; an extra wrong wire stops the answer from being fully correct.

@section(Braille and 3-D printed Braille)

Show text as Braille and download it as a 3-D printable plate, or export the whole document as Braille.

@braille(Hello)

@b{The whole document.} @@braille() with nothing inside, at the top, adds a button that converts the whole document to Braille and downloads the embossed STL.

@braille()

@b{A dictionary equation.} eq= brailles an entry's stored caption (or its spoken reading when it has none).

@braille(eq=med.bl)

@b{Graded.} Not graded: it is content, and a student sees and uses exactly what you see.


@section(Every other argument)

@b{Flashcard extras.} theme= sets the look, width= and height= size it, time= is the live-class window.

@flashcards(id=units-deck, theme=dark, width=500, height=260, time=5m)
volt :: joule per coulomb
ampere :: coulomb per second
@end

@b{Module extras.} base= says where the modules are served from, width= and height= size the frame.

@module(kmap, base=https://knowsy.duckdns.org, width=100%, height=520)

@b{Picture questions by argument.} prompt= is the question text, pts= its points, answer= the same as targets=, file= the older spelling of src=, width= and height= size the picture.

@hotspot(file=teaching-widgets.assets/circuit.svg, answer=#R1, prompt=Click R1., pts=2, width=400, height=240)
@end

@dragdrop(file=teaching-widgets.assets/circuit.svg, prompt=Label the resistors., pts=2, width=400, height=240)
R1 -> #R1
R2 -> #R2
@end

@connect(file=teaching-widgets.assets/map.svg, prompt=Join a to b., pts=1, width=400, height=240)
#a - #b
@end
